Method 1️⃣: Brute Force
Idea
We calculate the altitude for every point from scratch.
For each index i, we recompute the sum of all previous gains 0 → i-1.
Then we track the maximum altitude.

This is very direct but inefficient because we repeat the same work again and again.

Approach
Step 1: Understand starting point
We start at altitude = 0
There are n + 1 points total

Step 2: Compute altitude for each point
For every index i (from 0 to n):

Set alt = 0
Go back from 0 → i-1
Add all values of gain[j]
This gives altitude at point i

Step 3: Track maximum altitude
After calculating altitude for point i
Compare it with current maximum
Update maximum if needed

Step 4: Repeat for all points
Do this for all i = 0 → n
Final answer is the highest altitude seen

Why it works
Every point is independently calculated using the definition:

altitude[i] = sum of gain[0 → i-1]

So correctness is guaranteed, but efficiency is not.

CODE:
class Solution {
    public int largestAltitude(int[] gain) {
        int n = gain.length;
        int mx = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            int alt = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                alt += gain[j];
            }
            mx = Math.max(mx, alt);
        }

        return mx;
    }
}
Time Complexity: O(n²) (nested loops)
Space Complexity: O(1)
